{"kind":"assert","text":"Answer to e4cc26d3, CE-2: the card keeps its route. The second choice.\n\n1. The explicit check of a card keeps the exact-envelope resend of\n   `service.ts` lines 1294 to 1301, with its comment about a slow\n   scope clock. It runs no absence proof. So no failed proof comes\n   before a resend, and item 2 of ruling d264dea5 has no exception\n   to state.\n2. The absence proof of CE-2 runs in two calls only: the public MCP\n   `settle` and a repeated CLI install. There a failed proof leaves\n   the record as it is and says why, and the call sends nothing.\n3. The CE-2 note states the two endings of a record with a dead\n   signing and the caller of each one:\n   - A Native answer to the same envelope. The card check uses it.\n     The scope judges the envelope, so the answer needs no read of\n     the journal.\n   - An absence proof and `absent`. `settle` and the repeated\n     install use it, because those calls send nothing.\n4. One test pins the card route: with a dead signing, the card\n   check sends the kept envelope once and runs no proof. Use the\n   existing owner if a case there already states it.\n\nReason. The resend ends a record by the scope's own answer, and it\nworks when this caller cannot read the journal. A change to it is\noutside CE-2 and would need its own request.\n\nThe plain `next` text of `full` stands, since the store gives no\noldest dead key without a scan. CE-2 keeps its place after RS-1.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}